# branding
- The TraceQuest horizontal wordmark uses expressive flared serif lettering with readable shared-stroke ligatures @logo-design @implemented
- The primary square TraceQuest logo is the simplified TQ ligature with an open Q curl, joined top stroke, and flared serifs matching the wordmark @logo-square @implemented
- The TraceQuest browser icon is supplied as a multi-resolution ICO with 16, 32, and 48 pixel images, preserving a recognisable TQ silhouette at browser-tab sizes @logo-favicon @implemented
- TraceQuest provides reusable path-based SVG and PNG versions of its wordmark and primary TQ mark, plus a multi-resolution ICO, from one shipped asset directory @branding-integration @implemented
- The README and app headers use the selected TraceQuest identity, adapting to light and dark themes, and all served pages and standalone reports include the TQ favicon @branding-integration @implemented
- Brand asset URLs return intact image bytes in regular and read-only hub mode, and packaged installations include every runtime brand asset @branding-integration @implemented
- Every README product screenshot and UI example is rendered from the current TraceQuest interface with the canonical TQ mark and wordmark; screenshot refresh checks the displayed brand before capture @spec @branding-media @implemented
- Documentation recordings use the current identity, and historical regression images show the relevant evidence without obsolete app branding @spec @branding-media @implemented

# search
- label: next to search.idx the search index is also written as search.bin, a binary copy that a later process opens without parsing the whole index and from which it decodes only the posting lists its query looks up; an index opened from search.bin gives the same words, sessions, word counts, document lengths and iteration orders as the same index loaded from search.idx, before and after it is changed, so search and handoff rank exactly as before; a search.bin that is from another format version or no longer matches search.idx (rewritten, replaced or corrupted) is ignored, the index is loaded from search.idx and search.bin is rewritten
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'binary search index' test/bin/search-index-binary.test.js
  tags: [spec, search-perf, cli-search, implemented]
- label: opening a 3,000-session search index from search.bin and looking up a three-word query (with prefix expansion of the last word) takes under a tenth of the time of loading the same index from search.idx
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'search.bin opens fast' test/bin/search-index-binary.test.js
  tags: [spec, search-perf, cli-search, implemented]
- label: a process that opened the search index from search.bin and never changed it does not rewrite search.idx or search.bin when it flushes its deferred writes
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'unchanged index opened from search.bin' test/bin/search-index-binary.test.js
  tags: [spec, search-perf, cli-search, implemented]
- label: when the sidecar is available, the near-words re-rank of tracequest search and tracequest handoff reads the candidate pool's session files (first 8 MB each) in one parallel sidecar near scan instead of in Node, and every session's near-words score is bit-identical to the JS scan's, including case variants, camelCase and prefix word boundaries, verbatim query copies, recorded searches, Codex injected lines, latin1 bytes and the 8 MB cap; OpenCode and dsh sessions, files the sidecar cannot read, and runs without the sidecar are scored by the JS scan with the same result
  command: npm run build:sidecar >/dev/null 2>&1 && TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/bin/near-sidecar-scan.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-search, search-perf, search-ranking, implemented]
- label: the sidecar near subcommand reads one JSON request on stdin (needles as hex bytes, weights as f64 bit patterns, the verbatim query as UTF-16 code units) and prints per file [best run weight, counted runs, ran a search for the query] or null when the file cannot be read
  command: cd sidecar && cargo test near_
  tags: [spec, search-perf, sidecar, implemented]
- label: when the sidecar indexes sessions it still writes their index.json metadata, but the search terms (words and counts) that search.idx stores for each session it re-read, in a full or a delta run, are the ones the JS indexer stores for that session without the sidecar (for example the text of a long Claude tool result, which the sidecar's own terms leave out), so search and handoff find and rank the same sessions with and without the sidecar
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'sidecar index search terms' test/bin/sidecar-index-terms.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-search, handoff, search-ranking, sidecar, sidecar-parity, implemented]
- label: a search.idx of format 4, whose sessions can hold the sidecar's own search terms, is not used: the next search or handoff rebuilds it as format 5 with the JS indexer's terms, with or without the sidecar
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'search index format' test/bin/sidecar-index-terms.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-search, handoff, search-ranking, sidecar, sidecar-parity, implemented]
